en, connecting the two islands Djurgården and Beckholmen. Originally built in 1848–1858 when the first docks were constructed on Beckholmen, Beckholmsbron is the only remaining wooden bridge in Stockholm still used by heavy vehicles. It was repaired several times before the Royal Djurgården Administration had it rebuilt in 1992.","wpu":"Beckholmsbron","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Bron_till_Beckholmen.jpg/960px-Bron_till_Beckholmen.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Beckholmen","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":59.320833,"lo":18.1,"d":418,"ds":"Beckholmen (Swedish: \"Pitch Islet\") is a small island in central Stockholm, Sweden.","wpu":"Beckholmen","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a6/Beckholmen.JPG/960px-Beckholmen.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Baltic Sea Science Center","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":59.324,"lo":18.104,"d":453,"ds":"The Baltic Sea Science Center in Stockholm is part of the open-air museum Skansen, and showcases the Baltic marine environment and its threats, using aquariums, exhibitions, and educational activities.","wpu":"Baltic Sea Science Center","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/30/Baltic_Sea_Science_Center_in_Skansen_Stockholm_Sweden.jpg/960px-Baltic_Sea_Science_Center_in_Skansen_Stockholm_Sweden.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Galärvarvskyrkogården","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":59.328056,"lo":18.094167,"d":462,"ds":"Galärvarvskyrkogården (lit. 'Galley Shipyard Cemetery') is a cemetery on the island of Djurgården in Stockholm, Sweden. It has about 1,300 burial sites and an area of approximately 0.9 hectares.","wpu":"Galärvarvskyrkogården","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/Gal%C3%A4rvarvskykog%C3%A5rden_nov_2012b.jpg/960px-Gal%C3%A4rvarvskykog%C3%A5rden_nov_2012b.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Kastellholmsbron","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":59.323694,"lo":18.087639,"d":475,"ds":"Kastellholmsbron (Swedish: \"The Citadel Islet Bridge\") is a small bridge in central Stockholm, Sweden, connecting the small island Kastellholmen to Skeppsholmen. The name is derived from the citadel, lending its name to the entire island. The first citadel was constructed in the 1640s together with the bridge.","wpu":"Kastellholmsbron","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/61/Kastellholmsbron_2009.jpg/960px-Kastellholmsbron_2009.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Junibacken","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":59.329444,"lo":18.088889,"d":727,"ds":"Junibacken is a children’s attraction, founded by Staffan Götestam, Fredrik Uhrström and Peder Wallenberg. It is situated on the island of Djurgården in Stockholm, Sweden. It was officially opened by the Swedish royal family on 8 June 1996.","wpu":"Junibacken","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/79/Junibacken_2007.jpg/960px-Junibacken_2007.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Moderna Museet","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":59.326389,"lo":18.083611,"d":751,"ds":"Moderna Museet (lit. 'the modern museum') is a state museum for modern and contemporary art and design, located on the island of Skeppsholmen in central Stockholm, opened in 1958.","wpu":"Moderna Museet","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/47/MODERN_MUSEUM_MODERNA_MUSEET_STOCKHOLM_%2819%29.jpg/960px-MODERN_MUSEUM_MODERNA_MUSEET_STOCKHOLM_%2819%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Djurgårdsbron","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":59.331,"lo":18.094,"d":786,"ds":"Djurgårdsbron (Swedish: \"The Djurgården Bridge\") is a bridge in central Stockholm, Sweden. Designed by Carl Fraenell and built for the Stockholm World's Fair 1897, the present bridge forms a southern extension to the boulevard Narvavägen, thus connecting mainland Östermalm to the island Djurgården.","wpu":"Djurgårdsbron","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/34/Djurg%C3%A5rdsbron.jpg/960px-Djurg%C3%A5rdsbron.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Teater Galeasen","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":59.327222,"lo":18.083333,"d":802,"ds":"Teater Galeasen is an independent Swedish theatre group based at Skeppsholmen in Stockholm. It was founded in 1983. Teater Galeasen is known for their singular, avant-garde and experimental productions of both classic and contemporary dramatic work.","wpu":"Teater Galeasen","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/Teater_Galeasen.jpg/960px-Teater_Galeasen.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Skeppsholmen Church","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":59.326389,"lo":18.081944,"d":840,"ds":"The Skeppsholmen Church (Swedish: Skeppsholmskyrkan) is a former church on the islet of Skeppsholmen in central Stockholm, Sweden.","wpu":"Skeppsholmen Church","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/02/Skeppsholmskyrkan_i_Stockholm.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"Saltsjön","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":59.318653,"lo":18.107528,"d":883,"ds":"Saltsjön is a bay of the Baltic Sea that extends from Stockholm archipelago to the inner city of Stockholm. Its innermost part reaches the eastern shore of Gamla stan at Skeppsbrokajen. It is navigable for large craft and the major ferry lines to and from Stockholm pass through it.","wpu":"Saltsjön","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/aa/Stockholm%27s_Saltsjon_and_the_Viking_Line_terminal.jpg/960px-Stockholm%27s_Saltsjon_and_the_Viking_Line_terminal.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Bellmansro","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":59.324139,"lo":18.112189,"d":918,"ds":"Bellmansro was a restaurant north of Oakhill Palace on Djurgården island in Stockholm, which opened as a catering hall in 1828. The restaurant's eventual name reflected its proximity to Johan Niclas Byström's bust of Carl Michael Bellman that was dedicated on July 26, 1829. For much of its history Bellmansro was a tavern, but in the late 19th century it became a restaurant with food service.","wpu":"Bellmansro","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/Bellmansro_1940.jpg/960px-Bellmansro_1940.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Fotografiska","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":59.317786,"lo":18.084722,"d":941,"ds":"Fotografiska (Swedish for \"photographic\") is an international museum network that presents photography and art. Founded in 2010 in Stockholm, Fotografiska has locations in Stockholm, Tallinn, Berlin, and Shanghai. The museum presents a rotating program of temporary exhibitions by both established and emerging photographers - some of which tour globally across its locations.","wpu":"Fotografiska","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fd/Fotografiska_museet_Stockholm_2016_01.jpg/960px-Fotografiska_museet_Stockholm_2016_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Skeppsholmsbron","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":59.327222,"lo":18.079167,"d":1019,"ds":"Skeppsholmsbron (Swedish: \"The Skeppsholm Bridge\") is in central Stockholm, Sweden, connecting Blasieholmen to Skeppsholmen. The bridge, 165 metres long and 9.5 metres wide, consists of a 5.5 metre wide roadway flanked by 2 metre pathways, and has 5 arches. It was the first forged iron bridge to be constructed in Sweden, manufactured by Motala Verkstad in 1861.","wpu":"Skeppsholmsbron","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/Skeppsholmsbron_Stockholm_Crown_Strandvagen.jpg/960px-Skeppsholmsbron_Stockholm_Crown_Strandvagen.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"St Peter and St Sigfrid's Church","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":59.33264,"lo":18.1024,"d":1027,"ds":"St Peter and St Sigfrid's Church, often referred to locally as the English Church (Swedish: Engelska kyrkan), is an Anglican church in Stockholm, Sweden. It was built in the 1860s for the British congregation in the city and was originally located on Rörstrandsgatan (later renamed Wallingatan) in the Norrmalm district before being moved, stone by stone, to the Diplomatstaden area of Östermalm in 1913.","wpu":"St Peter and St Sigfrid's Church","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/Engelska_kyrkan%2C_Stockholm_2006..jpg/960px-Engelska_kyrkan%2C_Stockholm_2006..jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Oscar's Church","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":59.333333,"lo":18.093333,"d":1048,"ds":"Oscar's Church (Swedish: Oscarskyrkan) is a Church of Sweden church in Stockholm. The three-aisled hall church, which holds 1,200 people, has an 80-metre-high (260 ft) tower in the south-western part of the building. Oscar's Church is located in the south-eastern part of Östermalm, where Storgatan and Narvavägen meet, near the Swedish History Museum.","wpu":"Oscar's Church","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0c/Oscarskyrkan_Stockholm_2016_01.jpg/960px-Oscarskyrkan_Stockholm_2016_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Rosendals Trädgård","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":59.327219,"lo":18.113633,"d":1062,"ds":"Rosendals Trädgård is a garden open to the public situated on Djurgården, west of Rosendal Palace, in the central part of Stockholm, Sweden. Today, Rosendals Trädgård is open to public visitors in order to let visitors experience nature and to demonstrate different cultural effects on gardening through history. The purpose is to practise biodynamic agriculture and pedagogical education.","wpu":"Rosendals Trädgård","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/Rosendals_tr%C3%A4dg%C3%A5rd.jpg/960px-Rosendals_tr%C3%A4dg%C3%A5rd.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Berwald Hall","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":59.333333,"lo":18.100556,"d":1069,"ds":"The Berwald Hall (Swedish: Berwaldhallen) is a concert hall situated in a park landscape at Dag Hammarskjölds väg 3 in the Östermalm district of Stockholm, Sweden. Construction on the building began in 1976 based on a design by architects Erik Ahnborg and Sune Lindström. The hall is shaped as a hexagon.","wpu":"Berwald Hall","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/72/Berwaldhallen_2008.jpg/960px-Berwaldhallen_2008.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Djurgårdsbrunnsviken","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":59.331111,"lo":18.108889,"d":1076,"ds":"Djurgårdsbrunnsviken is a bay in central Stockholm, Sweden, together with the canal Djurgårdsbrunnskanalen forming the northern shore line of the island Djurgården (or more correctly between Northern and Southern Djurgården). The bridge Djurgårdsbron stretches over the bay.","wpu":"Djurgårdsbrunnsviken","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d6/Kakn%C3%A4stornet_Djurg%C3%A5rdsbrunnsviken_Stockholm_Sweden_20050812.JPG/960px-Kakn%C3%A4stornet_Djurg%C3%A5rdsbrunnsviken_Stockholm_Sweden_20050812.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Stockholms ström","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":59.326281,"lo":18.077339,"d":1088,"ds":"Stockholms ström (The Stream of Stockholm), also known as Strömmen (The Stream), in Stockholm is the innermost part of Saltsjön, a bay of the Baltic Sea. It continues into Lake Mälaren through Norrström and Söderström.","wpu":"Stockholms ström","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c7/Norrstr%C3%B6m_December_2012_02.jpg/960px-Norrstr%C3%B6m_December_2012_02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Stiftelsen Musikkulturens Främjande","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":59.33265,"lo":18.08617,"d":1111,"ds":"Stiftelsen Musikkulturens Främjande (English: The Foundation for the Furthering of Music Culture) or The Nydahl Collection is a musical museum and an archive of musical manuscripts and letters, located in Stockholm. It was established in 1920 by Captain Rudolf Nydahl (1882-1973) to manage the collections of musical instruments, manuscripts and other objects related to music that he built up during his lifetime.","wpu":"Stiftelsen Musikkulturens Främjande","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Stiftelsen_Musikkulturens_Fr%C3%A4mjande.jpg/960px-Stiftelsen_Musikkulturens_Fr%C3%A4mjande.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Nationalmuseum","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":59.3286,"lo":18.0781,"d":1136,"ds":"Nationalmuseum is the national gallery of fine arts of Sweden, located on the peninsula Blasieholmen in central Stockholm. The museum's operations stretch far beyond the borders of Blasieholmen, including the National Portrait Gallery collection at Gripshom, the Gustavsberg porcelain museum, several castle collections and the Swedish Institute in Paris (Institut Tessin).","wpu":"Nationalmuseum","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fd/Nationalmuseum_July_2025.jpg/960px-Nationalmuseum_July_2025.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Stora Hoparegränd","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":59.324722,"lo":18.075,"d":1194,"ds":"Stora Hoparegränd is an alley in Gamla stan, the old town of Stockholm, Sweden. Connecting Skeppsbron to Österlånggatan, it forms a parallel street to Skottgränd and Drakens gränd. It appears in historical records as Mechiill hopares grennd (1550), Hopers grändh (1578), Michel Hopers grendh (1582) and St.","wpu":"Stora Hoparegränd","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/Store_Hoparegr%C3%A4nd.JPG/960px-Store_Hoparegr%C3%A4nd.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"}],"about":{"ds":"Stockholm (; Swedish: [ˈstɔ̂kː(h)ɔlm] ) is the capital and most populous city of Sweden, as well as the largest urban area in the Nordic countries. Approximately 1 million people live in the municipality, with 1.6 million in the urban area, and 2.5 million in the metropolitan area. The city stretches across fourteen islands where Lake Mälaren flows into the Baltic Sea.","wpu":"Stockholm","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Royal_Dramatic_Theatre_Stockholm.jpg/960px-Royal_Dramatic_Theatre_Stockholm.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},"wk":"2026-10-18"}